</image> -->
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>y=f(x)=x^{2}-2</m>.
                Find the average rate of change of <m>y</m> between <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We use the definition of the average rate of change with <m>a=1</m>, <m>b=3</m> and <m>y=f(x)=x^{2}-2</m>:
                <md>
                    \dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}=\dfrac{f(3)-f(1)}{3-1}=\dfrac{(3^2-2)-(1^2-2)}{3-1}=\dfrac{7-(-1)}{2}=\dfrac{8}{2}=4
                </md>
                Therefore, the average rate of change of <m>y</m> between <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=3</m> is <m>4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
        This is not an applied example so <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> don't have real-life units.
        In general, the average rate of change <m>\dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}</m> is measured in
        <md>
            \text{units of }\aroc = \frac{\text{units for } y}{\text{unit for } x}
        </md>
        Were units associated with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in the last example, we would write that the average rate of change of <m>y</m> between <m>x=1</m> and <m>x=3</m> is <m>4 \; \frac{\text{units for } y}{\text{units for } x}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Let's revisit <xref ref="example-ch1-nicotine"/> about nicotine leaving the body after a cigarette is smoked.
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The amount of nicotine in a person's bloodstream, <m>N = f (t)</m>, in milligrams, is a function of the time <m>t</m>, in hours, that has passed since the person smoked a single cigarette.
                The graph of the function <m>f(t)</m> is shown below.
            </p>

            <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_5_4">
                <shortdescription>A short description.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image label="1_5_4_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=0,xmax=7.5,
ymin=0,ymax=2.5,
xtick={0,...,8},
ytick={0.5,1,1.5,2},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(N\),
x post scale = 1
]
\addplot[domain=0:7] {2*(0.5)^(x/2)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
                Use the graph to find and interpret the average rate of change of <m>N</m> between:
                <ol>
                    <li><m>t=0</m> and <m>t=4</m>;</li>
                    <li><m>t=2</m> and <m>t=4</m>.</li>
                </ol>
                Include units with your answers.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            To find the average rate of change between <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=4</m>, we use the graph to find the values of the function <m>f(t)</m> at <m>t=0</m> and at <m>t=4</m>.
                            At <m>t=0</m>, we have <m>N=f(0) = 2</m> milligrams.
                            At <m>t=4</m>, <m>N = f(4)= 0.5</m> milligrams.
                            Thus:
                            <md>
                                \aroc =\dfrac{\Delta N}{\Delta t}=\dfrac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0}=\dfrac{0.5-2}{4-0}=\dfrac{-1.5}{4}=-0.375.
                            </md>
                            Here the variables are associated with units: <m>N</m> is measured in milligrams while <m>t</m> is measured in hours.
                            Hence, <m>\Delta N</m> is measured in milligrams and <m>\Delta t</m> is measured in hours.
                            The average rate of change has units <m>\frac{\text{milligrams}}{\text{hour}}</m> which can be written as <m>\frac{\text{mg}}{\text{hr}}</m>.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Therefore, the average rate of change of <m>N</m> between <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=4</m> is
                            <md>
                                -0.375 \, \frac{\text{mg}}{\text{hr}}.
                            </md>
                            The rate is negative as the amount of nicotine decreased between <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=4</m>.
                            For the interpretation, we would say that on the interval <m>0\leq t \leq 4</m>, nicotine leaves the body at the average rate of <m>0.375</m> milligrams per hour.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We need values <m>f(2)</m> and <m>f(4)</m>, which are obtained from the graph: <m>f(2)=1</m> and <m>f(4)=0.5</m>.
                            Hence,
                            <md>
                                \aroc =\dfrac{\Delta N}{\Delta t}=\dfrac{f(4)-f(2)}{4-2}=\dfrac{0.5-1}{2}=\dfrac{-0.5}{2}=-0.25 \, \frac{\text{mg}}{\text{hr}}.
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            On the interval <m>2\leq t \leq 4</m>, nicotine leaves the body at the average rate of <m>0.25</m> milligrams per hour.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A population <m>P</m> of a small village at time <m>t</m>, measured in years since the year 2010, is given by <m>P=P(t)</m>.
                Here are the annual readings:
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-ch1-village">
                <title/>
                <tabular left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor" top="minor" halign="center">
                    <row>
                        <cell><m>t</m> (years)</cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>5</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>7</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>9</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>10</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>P(t)</m> (people)</cell>
                        <cell><m>150</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>160</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>140</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>180</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>210</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>200</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>250</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>290</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>300</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>350</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>400</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the change in the population <m>\Delta P</m> between <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=6</m>.
                            What are the units of <m>\Delta P</m>?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the average rate of change in the population between <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=6</m>.
                            What are the units of this rate of change?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The change in <m>P</m> between <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=6</m>, <m>\Delta P</m>, tells us by how much the population changed from <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=6</m>:
                            <md>
                                \Delta P=P(6)-P(2)=250-140=110.
                            </md>
                            The population increased by <m>110</m> people from <m>t=2</m> to <m>t=6</m> (from 2012 to 2016).
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The average <alert>rate</alert> of change tells us how fast the population was changing between <m>t=2</m> and <m>t=6</m> and is measured in people/year:
                            <md>
                                \aroc =\dfrac{\Delta P}{\Delta t}=\dfrac{P(6)-P(2)}{6-2}=\dfrac{250-140}{6-2}=\dfrac{110}{4}= 27.5 \, \frac{\text{people}}{\text{year}}.
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A ball is dropped from the rooftop of a building 150 feet tall.
                The height of the ball above the ground, <m>h(t)</m>, in feet, <m>t</m> seconds after the ball is dropped is:
                <md>
                    h(t)=150-16t^{2}.
                </md>

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find the average rate of change of <m>h(t)</m> between <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=2</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            When will the ball hit the ground?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We use the definition of the average rate of change:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\aroc \amp \;=\; \dfrac{\Delta h}{\Delta t}\;=\; \dfrac{h(2)-h(0)}{2-0} </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp \;=\; \dfrac{(150-16\cdot 2^2)-(150-16\cdot 0^2)}{2}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp \;=\; \dfrac{86-150}{2}= -32 \, \frac{\text{ft}}{\text{sec}}</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            This can also be thought of as the average velocity of the ball between <m>t=0</m> and <m>t=2</m>.
                            The velocity is negative because the height is decreasing.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The ball hits the ground when it falls to a height of <m>0</m> feet, so we solve the equation <m>h(t)=150-16t^{2}=0</m> for <m>t</m>:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>150-16t^{2} \amp \; = \; 0 </mrow>
                                <mrow>150 \amp \; = \; 16t^{2}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\dfrac{150}{16} \amp \; = \; t^{2}</mrow>
                                <mrow>t^{2} \amp \; = \; 9.375 </mrow>
                                <mrow>t \amp \; = \; \pm\sqrt{9.375}</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            Mathematically, we get two solutions: <m>t=\sqrt{9.375}</m> and <m>t=-\sqrt{9.375}</m>.
                            However, only one makes sense in the applied context for our problem.
                            As <m>t</m> is the number of seconds after the ball is dropped, a negative answer does not make sense <mdash/> the ball can't hit the ground before it was dropped!
                            Hence, the ball hits the ground <m>t=\sqrt{9.375}\approx 3.062</m> seconds after it is dropped.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="exercises/ex_1_5.ptx"/>
</section>